<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<Title>TestFramework.LocalIO</Title>
		<Version>0.1.7</Version>
		<Authors>DeadMoon0</Authors>
		<Product>TestFramework</Product>
		<Description>Manage your Local IO with a Framework Timeline.</Description>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/DeadMoon0/TestFramework-LocalIO</PackageProjectUrl>
		<PackageIcon>Icon.png</PackageIcon>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/DeadMoon0/TestFramework-LocalIO.git</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageTags>Test;UnitTest;IntegrationTest;Framework;Fluent;Timeline;Local;IO</PackageTags>
		<PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
		<Copyright>Copyright (c) DeadMoon0 2026</Copyright>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>Multi-target net8.0 and net10.0.
Fixes, all of which failed silently before:
- Shell commands work on Linux and macOS. The command was passed to the shell as one argument string, so it was split on whitespace and only the first word ever ran - a multi-word command reported success and did nothing.
- A command producing more than a pipe buffer of output no longer deadlocks; both streams are drained while the process runs.
- A cancelled or timed-out command now has its process tree killed instead of being left running and still writing files after the run ended.
New:
- UseRunDirectory() gives a run its own directory and resolves relative paths against it, so concurrent runs stop sharing the process working directory.
- FileArtifactData.Content exposes the bytes without copying the whole file per access; Data is obsolete.
- A FileExists timeout now names the path it watched, and file artifact setup creates the parent directory it needs.
BREAKING:
- Discovered file artifacts are observed, not owned: a folder scan no longer schedules the files it finds for deletion at cleanup. Register an artifact explicitly to keep that behaviour.
- Cmd(command) resolves its working directory when the step runs rather than capturing it while the timeline is built.</PackageReleaseNotes>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<PropertyGroup>
		<PackageIconSourcePath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\Modules\TestFramework-Common\Assets\Icon.png</PackageIconSourcePath>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="$(PackageIconSourcePath)">
			<Pack>True</Pack>
			<PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
		</None>
	</ItemGroup>

	<!--
	  The package icon lives in the TestFramework-Common submodule. A clone that skips submodules
	  leaves it empty, and pack then fails with NuGet's opaque "icon file does not exist in the
	  package" / "Could not find a part of the path" error, which never mentions submodules.
	  Scoped to GenerateNuspec so ordinary builds and `dotnet test` stay unaffected.
	-->
	<Target Name="EnsurePackageIconSubmoduleIsInitialized" BeforeTargets="GenerateNuspec">
		<Error Condition="!Exists('$(PackageIconSourcePath)')"
		       Text="The package icon &quot;$(PackageIconSourcePath)&quot; is missing because the TestFramework-Common submodule is not checked out. Run 'git submodule update --init --recursive' in the repository root and pack again." />
	</Target>

	<ItemGroup>
	  <PackageReference Include="TestFramework.Core" Version="0.1.10" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="README.md">
			<Pack>True</Pack>
			<PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
		</None>
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
